<h2>The incident and the investigation</h2>
<p>On the night of 18 November 2024 the complainant was attacked with iron rods near his home, and a shot was allegedly fired at him; the MLC recorded grievous injuries. The petitioner was arrested within days, on 23 November 2024, after analysis of CCTV footage. Iron rods were recovered, and the complainant identified the petitioner in a test identification parade. The chargesheet followed in February 2025, and by the time the High Court heard the bail application, the Sessions case stood listed for arguments on charge on 20 November 2026.</p>

<h2>The rival arguments</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old">
    <h3>For the petitioner</h3>
    <p>Custody of almost two years without even charges being framed; the evidence is documentary and electronic, incapable of being tampered with; a TIP is merely corroborative and cannot anchor continued detention.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="arrow">&rarr;</div>
  <div class="col new">
    <h3>For the State and the victim</h3>
    <p>CCTV footage captures the assault; the TIP is positive; eyewitness statements under Section 180 BNSS and weapon recoveries complete the chain; the conduct shows co-ordinated unlawful-assembly action; and the trial is at the charge stage, not stalled.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2>How the Court weighed it</h2>
<p>Justice Pushkarna&rsquo;s reasoning followed the orthodox structure of bail adjudication. First, the prima facie assessment: the CCTV footage, the complainant&rsquo;s consistent account, the positive TIP and the recoveries together crossed the threshold. Second, the counterweights: the gravity of a co-ordinated armed assault with a firearm, and the live risk of witness tampering with the complainant and eyewitnesses yet to depose. Third, the custody factor: substantial, but not yet of the order that overwhelms everything else — particularly with the trial court seized of arguments on charge within weeks.</p>
<p>The Court anchored the approach in <em>Mahipal v. Rajesh Kumar</em> — including its reminder, drawn from <em>Ram Govind Upadhyay</em>, that bail discretion must be exercised on the nature of the accusation and the evidence — and in the recent restatement of the <em>Prahlad Singh Bhati</em> and <em>Kalyan Chandra Sarkar</em> factors in <em>State of Karnataka v. Sri Darshan</em>.</p>

<h2>Reading the order in practice</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Electronic evidence cuts both ways.</strong> The defence pressed that electronic evidence cannot be tampered with; the Court treated the same footage as the backbone of the prima facie case. Where the camera favours the prosecution, the &ldquo;untamperable evidence&rdquo; argument loses its force.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Custody arguments need a stalled trial.</strong> Prolonged-incarceration pleas succeed most often where proceedings have gone dormant. A case actively listed for arguments on charge gives the court a reason to let the trial run instead.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Co-accused orders matter.</strong> The Court noted the position of co-accused bail orders — parity arguments require careful mapping of roles, and a distinct role attributed to the applicant limits their value.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Doors remain open.</strong> Dismissal at this stage does not foreclose a fresh application after charge, after key witnesses are examined, or on genuine trial delay.</div>
</div>
<div class="note">The offences span the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 — including Section 109(1) (attempt to murder) and Section 3(5) (common intention) — with Sections 25/27 of the Arms Act. For readers mapping old law: Section 109 BNS corresponds to Section 307 IPC, and Section 3(5) BNS to Section 34 IPC.</div>
